\begin{aligned}
&100 = 9 \cdot 11 + 1, \\
&1 = 100 - 9 \cdot 11.
\end{aligned}
\] From the equation above, we see that $1 = 100 - 9 \cdot 11$. Hence, the modular inverse of 9 modulo 100 is $11$ because it satisfies $9 \cdot 11 \equiv 1 \pmod{100}$. The answer is $\boxed{11}$. </think> <answer>11</answer>

