\begin{align*}
100 &= 9 \cdot 11 + 1 \\
9 &= 1 \cdot 9 + 0
\end{align*}
\] From the first equation, we can express 1 as \[
1 = 100 - 9 \cdot 11
\] This tells us that the coefficient of 9, which is -11, is the modular inverse of 9 modulo 100 up to a sign. Since -11 is not in the range from 0 to 99, we add 100 to it to get the equivalent positive value. Therefore, \[
